Lab 6: Managing Cinder (Block Storage Service)
Objectives
Understand Cinder's role in OpenStack's block storage service
Learn to create, attach, and manage Cinder volumes
Configure volume types and storage backends
Monitor and troubleshoot Cinder services
Prerequisites
OpenStack environment with Cinder service installed
CLI access with OpenStack client tools (openstack, cinder)
Existing Nova instance for volume attachment
Admin privileges for backend configuration
Basic Linux command line knowledge
Lab Setup
SSH into your OpenStack controller node:
ssh username@controller
Source your OpenStack RC file:
source admin-openrc.sh
Task 1: Configure and Attach Cinder Volumes
Subtask 1.1: Create a Volume
List available volume types:

openstack volume type list
Expected Output: Shows available volume types (e.g., lvm, nfs)

Create a 1GB volume:

openstack volume create --size 1 --type lvm lab-volume
Key Concept: Volumes are created in GB increments and tied to a storage backend

Verify volume status:

openstack volume show lab-volume | grep status
Troubleshooting: If status stays 'creating', check cinder-scheduler logs

Subtask 1.2: Attach to Instance
List running instances:

openstack server list
Attach volume to instance:

openstack server add volume <instance-id> lab-volume
Verify attachment:

openstack volume show lab-volume -c attachments
Task 2: Manage Volume Types and Backends
Subtask 2.1: Create Custom Volume Type
Create new volume type:

openstack volume type create fast-ssd --description "High performance SSD"
Set extra specs (example for LVM backend):

openstack volume type set fast-ssd \
  --property volume_backend_name=lvmBackend \
  --property replication_enabled='<is> False'
Subtask 2.2: Configure Storage Backend
Edit Cinder config (as admin):

sudo vi /etc/cinder/cinder.conf
Add backend configuration (example for LVM):

[lvm]
volume_driver = cinder.volume.drivers.lvm.LVMVolumeDriver
volume_group = cinder-volumes
iscsi_protocol = iscsi
iscsi_helper = lioadm
Restart services:

sudo systemctl restart cinder-volume
Task 3: Monitor and Troubleshoot
Subtask 3.1: Service Status Check
List Cinder services:

openstack volume service list
Expected Outcome: All services should show 'up' state

Check specific service:

sudo systemctl status cinder-scheduler
Subtask 3.2: Log Analysis
View Cinder logs:

sudo tail -f /var/log/cinder/cinder-volume.log
Common error resolution:

# If volume fails to create:
sudo cinder reset-state --state available <volume-id>
Conclusion
In this lab you have:

Successfully created and attached Cinder volumes to instances
Configured custom volume types and storage backends
Monitored Cinder services and performed basic troubleshooting
Verification
Validate volume operations:

openstack volume list
openstack volume show <volume-id>
Confirm backend configuration:

openstack volume type list --long
Cleanup
Detach and delete volumes:
openstack server remove volume <instance-id> lab-volume
openstack volume delete lab-volume
